      "recommended_risk": "low",
      "technical_explanation": "The rule correlates network retrieval primitives with a presumed execution sink within the same file. Tracing the actual data flow in the supplied source: outbound fetch calls go only to the image-generation provider endpoints defined in the bundled provider registry, to the SillyTavern host for avatar retrieval, and to image URLs returned by those providers. Retrieved bytes are converted to base64 and used exclusively as image media for display, message attachment, and file saving through host utilities; the downloaded content never reaches eval, dynamic Function construction, script injection, or command execution. The byte-reassembly base64 helper and jQuery option construction are the likely sink-like tokens, and both are inert. User-entered provider keys are attached only as authorization headers to the matching selected provider, a flow the project README explicitly documents, including that direct-provider requests run in the browser. This matches the stated purpose of a chat-context image generation extension.",
      "layman_explanation": "The scanner noticed this extension both downloads data from the internet and contains something that looked like it could run code. Looking at what actually happens: it sends prompts to the AI image services the user configured, and any downloaded data is only ever a generated picture that gets displayed or saved. Nothing downloaded is ever executed as code, and the only data leaving the app goes to the image services the user picked, which is exactly what this tool is for.",
